"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][2]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M2-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M2-L1 – Quantities, Units and Physical Balances<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor: Mathematical, Physical and Computational Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Charge,\ncurrent, voltage and power<\/a>, powers of ten and basic arithmetic.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Convert quantities into\nconsistent SI units, check the dimensions of terms and determine an\nunknown quantity from an energy balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical Value, Unit and Dimension<\/h2>\n<p>A measured value such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>470<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">470\\,\\mathrm{\\mu F}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconsists of a numerical value and a unit. Substituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>470<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.70<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">470\\,\\mathrm{\\mu F}=4.70\\cdot10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the same physical quantity. The number alone, however, depends on\nthe choice of unit. For a resistance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe expected value in a purely numerical SI model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2200<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEntering\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead gives a resistance one thousand times too small.<\/p>\n<p>Dimensional symbols describe the nature of a quantity. For energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[W] = \\mathrm{M L^2 T^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes mass,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlength and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntime. The SI unit is the joule, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm J=\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPower\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=\\mathrm d W/\\mathrm d t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naccordingly has the unit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm W=\\mathrm{J/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn what follows, italic\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes energy, while upright\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the unit of power, the watt.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dimensional Checks in Calculations<\/h2>\n<p>A common physical unit allows us to compare terms written using\ndifferent symbols. For a resistor,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=U I=U^2/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{\\Omega} = \\mathrm{V/A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{V^2/\\Omega}=\\mathrm{V\\,A}=\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe numerical value still depends on the quantities substituted. A\ndimensional check identifies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=U/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example, as incorrect, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{V/\\Omega}=\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a current.<\/p>\n<p>Only terms with the same dimension may be added. Adding two energies\nin joules and millijoules also requires conversion to a common unit. The\narguments of exponential functions must be dimensionless. In\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(-t/\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe time constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust therefore have the same dimension as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA dimensionally correct expression can nevertheless be physically wrong.\nBoth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=UI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=2UI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor instance, have the unit watt. The applicable model determines which\nrelation holds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Energy Flow and Storage<\/h2>\n<p>First choose a system boundary and count energy entering and leaving\nas positive magnitudes. For an electrical test model with energy\nstorage, useful output and heat dissipation, we have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta W_\\mathrm{stored}=W_\\mathrm{in}-W_\\mathrm{useful}-W_\\mathrm{heat}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Under the usual passive sign convention for a single component,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=ui<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis positive when the current enters the positive voltage terminal.\nTemporarily negative power in an energy-storage component may indicate\nthe return of previously absorbed energy. An instantaneous power balance\nand an energy balance over a time interval therefore answer different\nquestions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example<\/h2>\n<p>A simplified device draws, over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDuring this time it delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.6\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the output and increases its stored energy by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDetermine the input energy and the heat released. After converting the\ncurrent, we obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.060<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{in}=5\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot0.060\\,\\mathrm A=0.30\\,\\mathrm W,\\qquad\nW_\\mathrm{in}=0.30\\,\\mathrm W\\cdot30\\,\\mathrm s=9.0\\,\\mathrm J.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The heat released is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9.0\\,\\mathrm J-6.6\\,\\mathrm J-0.4\\,\\mathrm J=2.0\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe output receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9.0<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>73.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.6/9.0\\approx73.3\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the input energy. The stored\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis accounted for separately over this interval. The mean thermal power\nis approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>66.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">66.7\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts magnitude is consistent with the input power of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm{mW}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_116_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Device energy balance with 9 joules of input energy, 6.6 joules of useful energy, 2 joules of heat released and a 0.4-joule increase in stored energy.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Of 9.0 J supplied to the device, 6.6 J leave as\nuseful energy and 2.0 J as heat; a further 0.4 J increases stored\nenergy.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The balance diagram distinguishes energy flows across the system\nboundary from the change in stored energy. The lower bar shows the\nfractions of the input over the entire time interval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and Solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> In the ideal model, a capacitor of\ncapacitance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>470<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">470\\,\\mathrm{\\mu F}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis charged to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHow much energy is stored in its field according to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>C<\/mi><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_C=\\tfrac12 C U^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nCheck the dimension of the formula. Also convert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm{Wh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto joules.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> We have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm F=\\mathrm{C/V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{F\\,V^2}=\\mathrm{C\\,V}=\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe field energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>470<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.019035<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12\\cdot470\\cdot10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm F\\cdot(9\\,\\mathrm V)^2=0.019035\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>19.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">19.0\\,\\mathrm{mJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm h=3600\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm{Wh}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>720<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">720\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Limitations and Further Learning<\/h2>\n<p>For time-dependent quantities, integrate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe product of two separately averaged values does not generally yield\nmean power. Heat crossing the system boundary and internal energy stored\nin the device are distinct terms. Which contributions belong in the\nbalance follows from the chosen boundary and observation period.<\/p>\n<p>In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a>,\nyou apply the balance principle to currents and check a circuit solution\nusing its residual.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.bipm.org/en/publications/si-brochure/\">BIPM,\nThe International System of Units, 9th edition<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · SI units, prefixes\nand quantity calculus. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-3-energy-stored-in-a-capacitor\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 8.3 – Energy Stored in a Capacitor<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 8.3 – field energy of an idealised capacitor. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M2-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M2-L2 – Systems of Linear Equations and Matrices<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor: Mathematical, Physical and Computational Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Kirchhoff’s\nlaws and systematic circuit analysis<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">quantities\nand units<\/a>. General concepts of linear maps are covered in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">University\nMathematics M1-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Construct a system of linear\nequations from node balances, solve it by elimination and check the\nsolution using the remaining current imbalance at each node.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>One Row per Node<\/h2>\n<p>At a node of unknown potential, the balance requires that the sum of\ncurrents leaving through resistors equal the injected current. If a\nreference node is set to zero volts, a conductance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto that reference contributes to the diagonal of the node’s matrix. If a\nbranch connects two unknown nodes, each balance also contains the\ncoefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmultiplying the other node potential. The negative term follows directly\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=g(v_1-v_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach row of the model thus represents a current balance rather than\nmerely a calculation rule.<\/p>\n<p>In this example, a conductance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconnects nodes A and B. From A to the reference there is a further\nconductance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand from B to the reference one of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo ideal sources inject\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto A and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto B. We perform numerical calculations with conductances in\nmillisiemens, voltages in volts and currents in milliamperes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_005_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two nodes A and B supplied by ideal sources and connected by a conductance of 1 mS. From A to the reference the conductance is 1 mS; from B to the reference it is 2 mS.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Two current sources feed nodes A and B, connected by\na conductance; branches to the reference node allow two nodal\ncurrent-balance equations.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Matrix and Elimination<\/h2>\n<p>In the order A, B,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Gv=b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;-1\\\\-1&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}v_A\\\\v_B\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The first row is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_A+(v_A-v_B)=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the second is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2v_B+(v_B-v_A)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAdd half the first row to the second. It then becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5<\/mn><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.5v_B=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_B=1.2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, by back-substitution,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_A=2.6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first node equation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.6+1.4=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin milliamperes. At the second node,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntowards the reference and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.4\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom B to A exactly balance the injected current of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The choice of calculation order should be distinguished from circuit\ntopology. The nodes can be reordered provided the columns of unknowns\nand the rows of balances are reordered consistently. In larger numerical\nmodels, the system is solved using a suitable factorisation. Explicitly\ncalculating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not necessary for this task.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Residual as an Error Indicator<\/h2>\n<p>For an approximate vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe measure the unbalanced injection using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=b-G\\hat v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>v<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat v=(2.5,1.2)^\\mathsf T\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3.8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1.1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}\n-\\begin{pmatrix}3.8\\\\1.1\\end{pmatrix}\n=\\begin{pmatrix}0.2\\\\-0.1\\end{pmatrix}\\,\\mathrm{mA}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At node A, the outward current is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntoo small; at node B, the calculated outward current exceeds the\ninjection by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe maximum norm\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|r\\|_\\infty=0.2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindicates the largest deviation in any row. For quantities on very\ndifferent scales or an ill-conditioned system, this measure alone does\nnot establish that the voltages are accurate. It checks whether the\ncalculated voltages satisfy the stated model. A modelling error can\nremain even when the residual vanishes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and Solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Reduce the conductance from B to the\nreference to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mS}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll other conductances and injected currents remain unchanged. Determine\nboth potentials and check the current in the connecting branch.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> In\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nonly the second diagonal entry changes from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2 v_A-v_B=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-v_A+2v_B=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_A=3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_B=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA current of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows through the connection from A to B. At the second node,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows to the reference while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\narrives from A. The external injection of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncloses the balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Limitations and Further Learning<\/h2>\n<p>The example uses resistive branches and establishes an unambiguous\nreference. A floating subcircuit without a potential reference can\nproduce a singular node equation even when voltage differences are\nuniquely determined. Nonlinear components require additional modelling.\nTime-dependent storage elements couple the node equations to\nderivatives. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>\nillustrates this transition.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-06sc-linear-algebra-fall-2011/pages/ax-b-and-the-four-subspaces/elimination-with-matrices/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 18.06SC – Elimination with Matrices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · elimination as\nan elementary row operation and matrix calculations. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-3-kirchhoffs-rules\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 10.3 – Kirchhoff’s Rules<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n10.3 – Kirchhoff’s current law and balance sign conventions. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M2-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M2-L3 – Calculus and Differential Equations for Time-Dependent Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor: Mathematical, Physical and Computational Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Capacitors,\ninductors and transient responses<\/a>, slope and the exponential\nfunction. Further mathematical detail is available in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">University\nMathematics M1-L9<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the instantaneous\nrate of change of a stored state from its accumulated change, and check\na linear differential equation using its initial value and a\nbalance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Derivative and Integral as Two Perspectives<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal capacitor of constant capacitance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=Cu_C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder the passive sign convention, positive current counts as charge\nentering the positive terminal. The rate of change is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_C=\\mathrm d q/\\mathrm d t=C\\,\\mathrm d u_C/\\mathrm d t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMultiplying the instantaneous voltage slope in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{V/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the unit ampere. The reverse calculation accumulates charge over a\ntime window:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>C<\/mi><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_C(t)=u_C(0)+\\frac{1}{C}\\int_0^t i_C(s)\\,\\mathrm d s.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The initial value is necessary because the same current profile\nproduces different state trajectories for different starting voltages.\nIn a discretisation, weighted rectangles or trapezoids approximate the\ncurrent integral. The differential equation, by contrast, contains the\nlocal rate of change. Large initial magnitudes of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlead to more rapid changes, provided\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Discharge through a Resistor<\/h2>\n<p>A capacitor with initial voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndischarges through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the shared upper terminal, branch currents are defined downwards.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_C+i_R=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_R=u_C/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe balance gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm d u_C}{\\mathrm d t} + \\frac{1}{RC} u_C=0,\n\\qquad u_C(0)=u_0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=RC<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMultiplying by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(t/\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nturns the left-hand side into the derivative of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(t/\\tau)u_C(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe product is therefore constant and initially equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_C(t)=u_0\\exp(-t/\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis derivation avoids dividing by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt therefore also holds for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand for negative initial values.<\/p>\n<p>As a check, differentiation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm d u_C/\\mathrm d t=-u_C/\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe solution agrees with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe state tends to zero as time increases. Each of these three checks\ncovers a different possible source of error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Balance<\/h2>\n<p>A capacitor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{\\mu F}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith an initial voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndischarges through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.2\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe time constant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.22<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.22\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInitially,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.73<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_C(0)=-6/2200\\,\\mathrm A\\approx-2.73\\,\\mathrm{mA}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nflows in the passive reference direction. The current magnitude\ndecreases with the voltage. After\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.11<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.11\\,\\mathrm s=\\tau/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe voltage is around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.64<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.64\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>By then, the charge change is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>236<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(6\\exp(-1/2)-6)\\,\\mathrm V\\approx-236\\,\\mathrm{\\mu C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegrating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_C(t)=-(u_0/R)\\exp(-t/\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields the same value, since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau/R=C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith positive capacitor voltage and negative capacitor current, the\nfield energy decreases. In the ideal branch, the same instantaneous\npower magnitude appears as power dissipated in the resistor. This\nbalance offers a check in addition to differentiation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_117_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponential discharge curve from 6 volts, with voltages marked after 0.11, 0.22 and 0.44 seconds, and the initial tangent.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: RC voltage decays exponentially from 6 V; after one\ntime constant of 0.22 s it is about 2.21 V, where the initial tangent\nmeets the time axis.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The initial tangent illustrates the initial rate of change. It\nintersects the time axis at one time constant; the actual discharge\ncurve, however, reaches zero only in the limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and Solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Calculate the voltage in the example after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.44<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.44\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHow long does it take for the voltage to drop to half its initial value\nfor the first time? What does the model predict for an initial voltage\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> After two time constants,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.812<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\exp(-2)\\,\\mathrm V\\approx0.812\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains. The half-life is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.152<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\ln 2\\approx0.152\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a negative initial value, the voltage remains negative at finite\ntimes and increases towards zero. Field energy depends on the square of\nthe voltage. Its time evolution is therefore identical for initial\nvalues of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Limitations and Further Learning<\/h2>\n<p>The exponential solution assumes constant values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn additional source changes the equilibrium value. For constant source\nvoltage, the deviation from the new equilibrium can then be chosen as\nthe state. Leakage currents, dependence on temperature and voltage, and\nadditional storage elements can alter the decay curve. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a>,\nyou will learn a representation suited to continuous sinusoidal\nexcitation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-5-rc-circuits\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 10.5 – RC Circuits<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 10.5 –\nfirst-order differential equations, discharge and time constant. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M2-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M2-L4 – Complex Numbers, Sinusoidal Functions and Phasors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor: Mathematical, Physical and Computational Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Sine, cosine and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">sinusoidal\nalternating current<\/a>. For the mathematical foundations, see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">University\nMathematics M2-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Represent an RMS phasor in\nCartesian and polar form, add phasors and convert their phase difference\ninto a time shift.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two forms of the same number<\/h2>\n<p>In electrical engineering, the imaginary unit is usually written as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes current. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=a+\\mathrm j b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm j^2=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe denote the angle relative to the positive real axis by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=r\\exp(\\mathrm j\\varphi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy Euler’s formula,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=r\\cos\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=r\\sin\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For the reverse conversion, a quadrant-aware function such as\nPython’s atan2 determines the angle from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arctan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arctan(b/a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone cannot distinguish between the first and third quadrants, for\nexample. It also fails when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe zero phasor has zero magnitude and no defined phase angle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Phasor and time-domain function<\/h2>\n<p>We use cosine as the reference and store the RMS value as the\nmagnitude of the phasor. From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=X_\\mathrm{eff}\\exp(\\mathrm j\\varphi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nat positive angular frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\pi f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\left(X\\exp(\\mathrm j\\omega t)\\right)\n=\\sqrt{2}X_\\mathrm{eff}\\cos(\\omega t+\\varphi).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A positive phase difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi_A-\\varphi_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeans in this convention that A leads B. If the frequencies are equal, a\nphase difference of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to a time difference of magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lvert\\Delta\\varphi\\rvert/\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe timing is unique only modulo whole periods. When calculating with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin radians per second, the angle must be expressed in radians. Degrees\nremain useful when presenting results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Adding phasors and comparing phases<\/h2>\n<p>Two voltages of the same frequency have RMS phasors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_A=(3+\\mathrm j4)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_B=(4-\\mathrm j3)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth magnitudes are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA lies in the first quadrant and B in the fourth. Their phases are\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>53.13<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">53.13^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mn>36.87<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-36.87^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Cartesian form is convenient for addition. The sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(7+\\mathrm j)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>50<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7.07<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{50}\\,\\mathrm V\\approx7.07\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand phase approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>8.13<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8.13^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAdding the magnitudes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirectly would be correct only for identical phases. Polar form is\nuseful for division because the magnitudes are divided and the angles\nsubtracted. Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_A/U_B=\\mathrm j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand A leads B by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis corresponds to a quarter of a period, or\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_006_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Phasor A ends at 3 plus 4 j volts, B at 4 minus 3 j volts, and their sum at 7 plus j volts. A dashed parallelogram illustrates the addition.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Adding phasors 3 + 4j V and 4 − 3j V geometrically\nor algebraically produces the resultant 7 + j V.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Express\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><msup><mn>135<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\exp(\\mathrm j135^\\circ)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin Cartesian form. A second RMS phasor has the same magnitude and phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDetermine the sum phasor and the phase difference.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The two phasors are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-\\sqrt2+\\mathrm j\\sqrt2)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\sqrt2+\\mathrm j\\sqrt2)\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe real parts cancel and the sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm j2\\sqrt2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand its phase is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first individual phasor also leads the second by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and further study<\/h2>\n<p>Phasors combine amplitude and phase at a fixed frequency. Signal\ncomponents of different frequencies are assigned separate phasors. Their\ntime-domain functions are then added. A single phasor also does not\ninclude a DC component or a transient response. Here the complex plane\nrepresents signals and their timing. Spatial direction and spatial\nderivatives, by contrast, are covered in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/precalculus-2e/pages/8-5-polar-form-of-complex-numbers\">OpenStax,\nPrecalculus 2e, § 8.5 – Polar Form of Complex Numbers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 8.5\n– Cartesian and polar representations and division in polar form. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/15-2-simple-ac-circuits\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 15.2 – Simple AC Circuits<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section\n15.2 – sinusoidal quantities, RMS values and phase relationships. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M2-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M2-L5 – Vector Calculus and Spatial Derivatives<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor: Mathematical, Physical and Computational Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Vector components and the dot\nproduct, as well as derivatives from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>. A\nvector field assigns a vector to each point in space. A scalar field\nassigns a single number to each point.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a field from a\npotential, interpret a field diagram and distinguish flux and\ncirculation by their surface and line contributions respectively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From potential to directional arrow<\/h2>\n<p>The gradient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof a scalar field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconsists of the partial derivatives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial\\phi/\\partial x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial\\phi/\\partial y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor each derivative, the other coordinate is held constant. The dot\nproduct\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\hat{\\boldsymbol s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the local rate of change in the unit direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒔<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\boldsymbol s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟎<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\phi\\ne\\boldsymbol 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe gradient points in the direction of steepest increase. At a regular\nlevel curve, it is perpendicular to the tangent.<\/p>\n<p>In the electrostatic model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E=-\\nabla\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the electric field. The minus sign indicates that the\npotential decreases in the direction of the field. A potential in volts\nyields field components in volts per metre.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Field diagram for a worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(x,y)=a(x^2-y^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=2\\,\\mathrm{V/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E(x,y)=(-2ax,\\,2ay).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe field is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-8,4)\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand its magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>80<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8.94<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{80}\\,\\mathrm{V/m}\\approx8.94\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong the horizontal axis, the arrows point towards the origin, and\nalong the vertical axis they point away from it. At the origin, the\ngradient is zero.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_007_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Arrow grid of the field E equal to minus 4 x in the x direction and plus 4 y in the y direction. Arrows pointing towards the origin on the x-axis and outwards on the y-axis form a saddle pattern.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The saddle-potential field points towards the origin\nalong the x-axis and away from it along the y-axis; magnitude and\ndirection depend on position.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Flux and circulation<\/h2>\n<p>Oriented <strong>flux<\/strong> through a surface sums the normal\ncomponent of the field. In the simple case of a constant field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a flat surface of area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi=A\\,\\boldsymbol F\\cdot\\hat{\\boldsymbol n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the field is parallel to the surface, this contribution vanishes. A\nuniform electric field vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,0,0)\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces, through a surface of area\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith its normal in the positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirection, a flux of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm{V\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReversing the normal reverses the sign. The flux density, or field\nquantity, and its integrated flux have different units.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Circulation<\/strong> sums the tangential component along a\nclosed, oriented curve. The integral is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∮<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\oint\\boldsymbol F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\boldsymbol r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA purely radial contribution on a circle is perpendicular to the path\nthere. A circulating field, by contrast, can produce a non-zero\nresult.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E=-\\nabla\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe integral along a path is the potential at the start minus the\npotential at the end. It therefore vanishes along a closed path. In the\nexample, the divergence of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis also zero because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial E_x/\\partial x=-2a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial E_y/\\partial y=2a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncancel each other. This does not mean the field vectors are zero. It\ndescribes a local balance between outgoing and incoming contributions of\nthe field.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Calculate the circulation of the\ncomparison field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol F=(-k y,k x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naround the square from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(L,L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntraversed in the positive anticlockwise direction, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=1\\,\\mathrm{V/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> On the bottom edge,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\non the left edge,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTravelling upwards along the right edge,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_y=kL<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the line integral gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kL^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong the top, the path goes to the left while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x=-kL<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe product of the field component and the directed displacement again\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kL^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2kL^2=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe comparison field is therefore not an electrostatic gradient field in\nthis region.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and further study<\/h2>\n<p>Arrow grids show values only at selected locations. Interpolation and\narrow length must be consistent with the actual field definition. The\nscalar potential example treats an electrostatic region. Time-dependent\nmagnetic flux can lead to electric field circulation and extends this\nmodel. Before studying field and wave models in M4, we examine the\nstatistical description of measured values in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-6-directional-derivatives-and-the-gradient\">OpenStax,\nCalculus Volume 3, § 4.6 – Directional Derivatives and the Gradient<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Section 4.6 – direction, gradient and level curves. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/6-2-line-integrals\">OpenStax,\nCalculus Volume 3, § 6.2 – Line Integrals<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 6.2 –\nintegration along oriented curves. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/6-5-divergence-and-curl\">OpenStax,\nCalculus Volume 3, § 6.5 – Divergence and Curl<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 6.5 – local\nfield balance and its relation to circulation. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M2-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M2-L6 – Probability and Statistical Modelling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor: Mathematical, Physical and Computational Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Fractions, sums and squares. The\nunderlying concepts are explored further in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">University\nMathematics M1-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the expected value and\nvariance of a discrete measurement model and determine which\nsimplifications are valid for independent samples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A measured value as a random variable<\/h2>\n<p>A hypothetical sensor produces an additive measurement error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat a constant input. In a simple discrete model, this error can take\nonly the values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet their probabilities be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir sum is one. The model describes a hypothetical distribution. A\nfinite list of actual observations would have its own relative\nfrequencies, which may differ from these probabilities.<\/p>\n<p>For possible values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand probabilities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe expected value is the weighted mean and the variance is the expected\nsquared deviation from that mean:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=\\operatorname{E}[X]=\\sum_i p_i x_i,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sum_i p_i(x_i-\\mu)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The expected value in this example is zero because the positive and\nnegative contributions cancel. The variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mV}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe standard deviation is its square root and is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.414<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.414\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLike\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit has a unit of voltage, whereas the variance has a squared voltage\nunit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Averaging and systematic offset<\/h2>\n<p>Consider four measurement errors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_1,\\dots,X_4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same distribution. If they are independent, their mutual\ncovariances vanish. For the mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar X=\\tfrac14\\sum_i X_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe therefore have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{E}[\\bar X]=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\operatorname{Var}(X)/4=0.5\\,\\mathrm{mV}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe standard deviation of the mean is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.707<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.707\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn general, under these assumptions it decreases as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>An undetected offset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshifts each measurement error and hence the expected value of the mean\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis constant contributes nothing to the variance about its own mean. In\nthis model, averaging therefore reduces random dispersion, but the\noffset remains. If, on the other hand, the four identical random errors\noccur in complete synchrony, their mean is the same error. In that case,\nthere is no reduction in dispersion.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_118_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two discrete probability distributions: a single measurement with a standard deviation of 1.414 millivolts and a mean of four independent measurements with 0.707 millivolts.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: For four independent readings in this example, the\nmean’s standard deviation is half that of a single reading, falling from\nabout 1.414 to 0.707 mV.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The distribution on the right results from all combinations of four\nindependent measurement errors in the model described above. It is more\nconcentrated around zero. The vertical lines represent individual\nprobabilities, not a continuous density.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Events in samples<\/h2>\n<p>A second model counts warnings. In each trial, the probability of a\nwarning is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the trials are independent. The probability of no warning in five\ntrials is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.59049<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-p)^5=0.59049<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe probability of at least one warning is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.40951<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.40951<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe probability of exactly one warning is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.32805<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5p(1-p)^4=0.32805<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause it can occur at five different positions.<\/p>\n<p>These products use two distinct assumptions. Independence permits\nmultiplication of the individual probabilities. A constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmakes every fixed arrangement with the same number of warnings equally\nprobable. Both assumptions should be justified separately when working\nwith a series of measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Determine the standard deviation of the\nmean of eight independent measurements in the first model. In the\nwarning model, what is the probability of at least one warning with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin three independent trials?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The variance of the mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/8=0.25\\,\\mathrm{mV}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the second part, the complementary event is three trials with no\nwarning, with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.98<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.941192<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.98^3=0.941192<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe required probability is thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.058808<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.058808<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.88<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.88\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and further study<\/h2>\n<p>Drift, shared disturbances and temporal filters can create\ndependencies between successive measurements. In particular, identical\nmeans and variances are not sufficient to determine the probabilities of\nrare events. A suitable distribution or an appropriate data-driven\nmethod is needed for that purpose. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a>,\nyou will document the inputs of a numerical experiment and check its\nresults.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/4-formula-review\">OpenStax,\nIntroductory Statistics 2e, Chapter 4 – Formula Review<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section Ch.\n4 Formula Review – expected value, variance and binomial distribution. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/7-1-the-central-limit-theorem-for-sample-means-averages\">OpenStax,\nIntroductory Statistics 2e, § 7.1 – The Central Limit Theorem for Sample\nMeans<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 7.1 – location and dispersion of the sample mean. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M2-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M2-L7 – Computational Experiments and Programming<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Bachelor: Mathematical, Physical and Computational Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Derivatives\nand differential equations<\/a>, loops and basic Python expressions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Solve a differential equation\nnumerically using the explicit Euler method and check the calculation\nagainst the initial value, an equilibrium case and an analytical\nreference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What must be reproducible?<\/h2>\n<p>A computational experiment consists of the physical model, the\ninputs, the numerical method and the evaluation criteria. Here, an ideal\ncapacitor is connected through a resistor to a constant source voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=RC<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm d u}{\\mathrm d t}=\\frac{U-u}{\\tau},\\qquad\nu(0)=u_0,\\qquad\nu_\\mathrm{ref}(t)=U+(u_0-U)\\exp(-t/\\tau).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The reference is the analytical solution of the same idealised model.\nWe choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=0.25\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a fixed comparison time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=0.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll times are entered as numerical values in seconds and voltages in\nvolts. This makes cause and effect traceable when a result changes with\nthe step size.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From model to program<\/h2>\n<p>The explicit Euler step uses the slope at the beginning of each\nstep:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{n+1}=u_n+h\\frac{U-u_n}{\\tau}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The difference from equilibrium satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{n+1}-U=(1-h/\\tau)(u_n-U)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor step sizes with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;h&lt;\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe discrete solution approaches equilibrium without a change of sign.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau&lt;h&lt;2\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit alternately overshoots and undershoots equilibrium and still\nconverges. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=2\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe magnitude of the deviation stays constant. This behaviour arises\nfrom discretisation and is not an oscillation in the continuous\ncapacitor model.<\/p>\n<p>The following code uses only the Python standard library. The step\nsize is determined by the fixed end time and number of steps. This\navoids shifting the end time by rounding the number of steps.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> exp, isclose<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> euler_endpoint(u_start, supply, tau, duration, steps):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> duration <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> steps<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    u <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> u_start<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(steps):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        u <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> h <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> (supply <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> u) <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> tau<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> u<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>supply <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">5.0<\/span>       <span class=\"co\"># V<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>tau <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.25<\/span>         <span class=\"co\"># s<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>duration <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span>     <span class=\"co\"># s<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u_start <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>      <span class=\"co\"># V<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>reference <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> supply <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> (u_start <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> supply) <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>duration <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> tau)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&quot;Reference: <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>reference<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> V&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>errors <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> steps <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    endpoint <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> euler_endpoint(u_start, supply, tau, duration, steps)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    error <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(endpoint <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> reference)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    errors.append(error)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&quot;h=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>duration <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> steps<span class=\"sc\">:.5f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> s, u(T)=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>endpoint<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> V, &quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>          <span class=\"ss\">f&quot;Error=<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>error<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> V&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> reference <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> supply<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> errors[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> errors[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> errors[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> isclose(euler_endpoint(u_start, supply, tau, <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>), u_start)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> isclose(euler_endpoint(supply, supply, tau, duration, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>), supply)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparing the results<\/h2>\n<p>The reference value at time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.323324<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.323324\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.125<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.125\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.687500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.687500\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0625<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.0625\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.499435<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.499435\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.03125<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.03125\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.409665<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.409665\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe absolute errors are approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.364176<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.364176\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.176112<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.176112\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.086341<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.086341\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe error is roughly halved when the step size is halved. In this\nexample, that is consistent with a first-order global error.<\/p>\n<p>The checks distinguish three cases. At an end time of zero, the\ninitial value must be preserved. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_0=U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe state must remain constant over time. For the transition between two\ndifferent states, the end value is additionally compared with the\nanalytical solution. All three tests refer to the chosen model.\nComparison with real measurement data would be a further step.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_119_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"RC charging curve as an analytical reference, with Euler sample points for four, eight and sixteen steps. The curves approach the reference as the step size decreases.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Euler approximations with four, eight and sixteen\nsteps approach the analytical RC charging curve as step size decreases;\nconnecting lines are visual guides.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The lines connect the discrete calculation points. The smaller the\nstep size in this example, the closer the numerical solution lies to the\nanalytical curve shown.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Exercise.<\/strong> Use just one Euler step for the\nobservation time of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the end value and explain why it is implausible for the\nidealised charging model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> The initial slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V/0.25\\,\\mathrm s=20\\,\\mathrm{V/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA step of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore leads to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStarting from zero, the exact charging curve approaches the constant\nsupply of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmonotonically from below. Overshooting it by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntherefore indicates an unsuitable numerical step. Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=2\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the deviation factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and further study<\/h2>\n<p>The code simulates an ideal storage element with one time constant.\nWith several widely differing time constants, an explicit method may\nrequire particularly small steps. Agreement at one point in time\nreplaces neither a check of the entire curve nor a validation of the\nmodel against independent observations. The <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Study\npathway<\/a> places the computational methods in the context of the rest\nof the course.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-2/pages/4-2-direction-fields-and-numerical-methods\">OpenStax,\nCalculus Volume 2, § 4.2 – Direction Fields and Numerical Methods<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 4.2 – explicit Euler method and comparison with analytical\nsolutions. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-5-rc-circuits\">OpenStax,\nUniversity Physics Volume 2, § 10.5 – RC Circuits<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section 10.5 –\ncapacitor charging and time constant. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
